dDiabetes Tracker Project

Home Page

* Welcome message
* Add Reading
* View History
* Weekly Report

⸻

Section 1: Flask

* render_template() tells Flask to find an HTML file in the templates folder and display it to the user.
* "/" is the home page route to the website.

Steps:

1. Import Flask.
2. Create a website called app.
3. When someone visits the home page (/), run the function home().
4. home() shows index.html.
5. If tracker.py is run directly, start the website.

⸻

Section 2: Create a Page to Enter Blood Glucose Readings

Create a button for the Add Reading page

<a href="/add">
    <button>Add Reading</button>
</a>

Add date

<label>Date</label>
<input type="date" name="date">

Add time

<label>Time</label>
<input type="time" name="time">

Add glucose

<label>Glucose</label>
<input type="number" name="glucose">

Save button

<input type="submit" value="Save Reading">

⸻

Section 3: Saving the Glucose Readings

* Open the file ("a" means append).
* Create a csv.writer object.
* Write the row into the CSV file.

file = open("glucose_reading.csv", "a")
writer = csv.writer(file)
writer.writerow([date, time, glucose])
file.close()

⸻

Section 4: Drawing the Graph and Table

* <canvas id="glucoseChart"></canvas> creates a blank area for the graph.
* <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js"></script> loads Chart.js.

⸻

Flask Navigation

# This directly shows the page
return render_template("data.html")
# Go visit the /data route.
return redirect("/data")

⸻

Notes

Browser
   ↓
Visits /
   ↓
Flask sees @app.route("/")
   ↓
Runs home()
   ↓
home() returns index.html
   ↓
Browser displays your HTML

HTML

* <h1> = Heading
* <table> = Table
* <tr> = Table row
* <th> = Table header
* <td> = Table data
* <a href=""> = Link

Jinja

* {{ }} = Display Python data
* {% for %} = Loop through data
* {% endfor %} = End loop

Technologies Used

1. Python
2. HTML
3. Flask
4. Jinja
5. Basic CSS
6. Small amount of JavaScript
7. Chart.js

⸻

Chart.js Notes

1. <canvas> creates a blank area for the graph.
2. document.getElementById() finds the canvas.
3. new Chart() creates the graph.
4. type chooses the graph style (line, bar, pie).
5. labels create the x-axis.
6. data creates the y-axis values.

⸻

JavaScript Reminder

* () → Do something
* [] → List of things
* {} → Description of things